        <h3 class="section-heading">Clinical Diagnostic Pathways</h3>
        <p>Because testosterone levels naturally fluctuate throughout the day, accurate diagnosis requires structured evaluation rather than a single ad-hoc check.</p>

        <table class="criteria-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Diagnostic Area</th>
              <th>Clinical Focus</th>
              <th>Why It Matters</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Morning Hormone Evaluation</strong></td>
              <td>Assesses peak physiological production levels</td>
              <td>Testosterone peaks early in the morning; late-day checks often yield unreliable results.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Pituitary Signaling Check</strong></td>
              <td>Differentiates between Primary and Secondary drivers</td>
              <td>Determines whether the issue originates in the testes or the brain-signaling axis.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Carrier Protein Assessment</strong></td>
              <td>Measures binding proteins like SHBG</td>
              <td>Clarifies how much free, biologically active hormone is accessible to your tissue cells.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Metabolic Screening</strong></td>
              <td>Evaluates baseline lipid, glucose, and cardiovascular markers</td>
              <td>Ensures underlying contributors like insulin resistance are addressed simultaneously.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

